        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "315fd6d8-2c9c-5be3-9f28-8e0bc8ae2fc2",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Kickapoo people, Cherokee Nation of Mexico, Anishinaabeg, Robert J. Conley\n- Places: Indiana, Mexico, Coahuila\n- Events: Indian removals in Indiana\n- Concepts: tribal recognition, indigenous peoples, name meanings, migratory patterns\n\nFacts:\n- Some Indian removals in Indiana occurred before 1830, but most removals in Indiana took place between 1830 and 1846.\n- The Kickapoo people are an Algonquian-speaking Native American and Indigenous Mexican tribe.\n- Anishinaabeg say the name Kickapoo means \"Stands here and there,\" and this meaning may have referred to the Kickapoo people's migratory patterns.\n- In the Anishinaabe language, the Kickapoo name is rendered as \"Giiwigaabaw,\" and its Kickapoo cognate is \"Kiwikapawa.\"\n- According to Robert J. Conley, the Cherokee Nation of Mexico is recognized by the state of Coahuila.\n- According to the Instituto Nacional de Lenguas Indígenas, the only indigenous people in Coahuila are the Kickapoo people.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "99733934-909f-51fb-b9fd-aa9c239e628f",
        "text": "Page: Indian removals in Indiana. Sentence: Some of the removals occurred prior to 1830, but most took place between 1830 and 1846.\nPage: Kickapoo people. Sentence: The Kickapoo people (Kickapoo: Kiikaapoa or Kiikaapoi) are an Algonquian-speaking Native American and Indigenous Mexican tribe. \"\nPage: Kickapoo people. Sentence: Anishinaabeg\" say the name \"Kickapoo\" (\"Giiwigaabaw\" in the Anishinaabe language and its Kickapoo cognate \"Kiwikapawa\") means \"Stands here and there,\" which may have referred to the tribe's migratory patterns.\nPage: Cherokee Nation of Mexico. Sentence: According to Robert J. Conley, the Cherokee Nation of Mexico is recognized by the state of Coahuilla; however, according to the Instituto Nacional de Lenguas Indígenas, which manages the official surveys of indigenous groups in Mexico, the only indigenous peoples in Coahuila are the Kickapoo people.",
